<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tzintzuntzan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tzintzuntzan was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purepecha-empire">Purépecha</a> (Tarascan) state on the north-eastern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-patzcuaro">Lake Pátzcuaro</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michoacan">Michoacán</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. By the 15th century it had become the political and religious centre of the kingdom, with tens of thousands of inhabitants and monumental yácata platforms that still stand on its terrace above the lake. It lost its role after the Spanish conquest of the 1520s, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patzcuaro">Pátzcuaro</a> became the seat of the new bishopric.</p></div>
